# Managed by MMX. The triage user may run exactly these commands as root,
# each with exactly these arguments; sudo refuses any other argument. Each
# one only reads, except mmwatchdog check-now --count-as-scheduled-run at
# the end, and none starts a pager or another program. The triage user
# reads the journal (journalctl), systemctl status, and the logs in
# /var/log without sudo, through the groups systemd-journal and adm.
#
# Left out on purpose:
# - journalctl as root: its options --vacuum-size and --cursor-file delete or
#   write files.
# - podman inspect: it shows a container's environment, which holds the
#   app's secrets. The containers log to the journal, so podman logs is not
#   needed either.

# Listening and connected sockets, with the programs that hold them.
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ss -tulpn
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ss -tupn
# The firewall's rules.
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ufw status verbose
# The WireGuard peers and their latest handshakes; private keys stay hidden.
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/wg show
# The containers and their state.
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/podman ps --all
# MMWatchdog's checks and recent events on this server.
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/mmwatchdog status --json
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/mmwatchdog events --json

# After a fix: a check group's scheduled runs, now, whose results count, so
# a fixed check's warning closes once the check has been healthy for its
# recovered_after_healthy_for, instead of at its next scheduled run, up to
# an hour later. It does nothing that MMWatchdog wouldn't do at those runs'
# own time, such as a restart that a failing check asks for. One line for
# each check group in mmwatchdog_checks/; MMWatchdog refuses a group that
# this server doesn't have. Never --without-daemon or --config-dir, which
# would run checks from any directory as root.
triage ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/mmwatchdog check-now systemd_units --count-as-scheduled-run
